Subject: RELIG/WACO: Farewell
Date: Fri, 5 Mar 1993 14:37:35

For various reasons I have decided to drop the list.  While waiting for the
plug to be pulled, I'd like to make a few comments.  I may be gone from the
list by the time you read this, so remarks meant for me would probably best
be sent to my email address.

I have greatly enjoyed my career as a semi-lurker on Extropians.  I am still
not sure whether I myself am an Extropian.  At least, I am a fellow traveller
with Extropian sympathies.  I think I could feel comfortable with most of you,
sardined into a highjacked starship. 

I have been listening to the swirling controversies about Waco, religions,
and the WTC bombing both in cyberspace and in my physical world.  I am some-
what disappointed.

I believe that human organizations and societies are much like biological
systems -- like organisms.  There is growth, change and transformation, or
there is stagnation and death.  In human societies, by definition, growth
and change originate with deviant splinters.  Some splinters are damaged
mutant sports and are stillborn or soon wither and die.  Others flourish.
>From where I stand, Both Extropians and Branch Davidians are deviant
splinters.  You have _that_ much in common.

I have to take the world as I find it.  It is not an anarchist utopia, and
human conflicts are not settled by PPL.  I doubt somewhat that I will ever
breathe truly free air in my lifetime.  With luck, I may budge the world an
inch or two in that direction.  A long journey is many steps.

The way I _do_ find my world is that I would be free, but my neighbors are
a brutish, intolerant lot.  I have First Amendment "rights", but if I pubicly
espouse ideas which my neighbors find strange and offensive, they will break
my windows under cover of night.  They will hang my pets from my porch, burn
crosses in my front yard, and make calls threatening my family.  Or sic the
state on me.

My reading of history is that this is a pretty universal story, common to most
times and most places.  What is different about our century is that there is
nowhere to go.  There is no frontier.  I see frontiers as a necessary safety
valve for societies, a relief for the frictive heat between main cultures and
their deviant splinters.  The frontier is the last refuge of the deviant;
where she may think and act and live her dreams without the persecution of
intolerant neighbors.

As I watched Waco unfold, I was put in mind of Puritans and Huguenots; of
Joseph Smith and his people, and their flight to god-forsaken Utah in the
1800s.

The real question (and possibly first step to walk), is where can we go?
Today, in the world as we find it, where is the sign reading: FRONTIER =>

So we have Waco.  A TV-world extravaganza.  Echos of a defiant James Cagney
holed up, holding all the cops in the world at bay.  Lunatic religious
fanatics, wild-eyed, pulling the goons of state down with them into a fire-
ball of Armageddon.  I'm not sure that the general public would strongly
differentiate between crazed Branch Davidians and robot-brain Extropians
starring in their living rooms in such a show.  Imagine the background
expose: armed anarchists high on brain drugs, trying to turn all us
god-fearing people into cyborgs and machines.  Yessir.

It seems to me that frontiers are the right and proper heritage of all
deviant splinters.  Should some new frontier make itself available, it is
likely that the nearest neighbors of an Extropian group will be Branch
Davidians or others of the deviant thought criminal ilk.  How will you get
along?

I had no joy in the sight of BATF storm troopers being shot.  I did not
feel like cheering.  I'm sure some were living caricatures of leering,
sadistic statist goons.  I'm also sure that many believe in what they
were doing.  These were children who recited the Pledge of Allegience
5 days a week for 12 years; who maybe went to church hand-in-hand with
their fathers and mothers; who were hammered with propaganda about the
glorious United States project; who got tears in their eyes and a lump in
their throats when they watched the flag go by in parades.  Don't think
for a second that I believe this makes them any less dangerous.  But
they _are_ products of their world, just as we are.  Do _you_ believe you
are in in charge of what _you_ are, what _you_ have become?  Why and how
did _you_ become what _you_ are?  Were you a mutant jack-in-the-box,
popping out of the soil of your family and community much to their
chagrin?  (I was.)  Or are you 2nd and 3rd generation anarchists and
libertarians?

Without access to frontiers, without some emergent tolerance, Waco is the
way the world will be for the forseeable future whenever aggressive deviant
splinters spring up.  In the world as _I_ find it, the state carries a
pretty big stick.  What are _you_ going to do?  Where are _you_ going to
go?

So long for now, Extropia.

--  James Emerson Hoburg
    hoburg@ultryx.com


