Date: Sat, 6 Mar 1993 05:09:28 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: BET: Living for another 50 years

Tim May:

>[50-50 odds to reach age 80, less than 10% to reach age 91]

I don't have numbers at hand, but those seem close to the statistics
for people reaching those ages today.  These  expectencies
are rapidly increasing, especially the life expectencies of getting
past the "85 hump" at age 60, since the "health consciousness" of 
the 70's and 80's.  Being male lowers expectancy a few years, but 
being high-income probably makes up most of that, and if you don't 
smoke you also can beat the averages quite a bit.

I think the following developments have a significant chance of
occuring:

* Various antioxidant substances are now known to increase the 
lifespan of fruitflies (both mean and max) by 50%.  Within the 
next 50 years I see a 50% chance that some sort of antioxidant 
cocktail can increase human mean & max lifespan by 25%. [Odds: 50%]

* Low-risk lifestyle (not smoking, avoiding HIV-risky behaviors,
low calorie/protein diet, high serum levels of vitamins C
and E and beta carotene, etc.) increase mean lifespan
by 25%.  [Odds: 100%]

* Heart disease, stroke, and cancer death rates continue to
fall with very early detection & eradication (eg with monoclonal
antibodies), increasing mean (but not max) lifespan 25% in 
the next 50 years. [odds: 50%]

* Gene therapy to fix strategic DNA damage, increasing
mean & max lifespan by at least 50% [Odds: 25%]

* Nanotechnology cell-repair machines reverse protein, DNA,
etc. damage, increasing mean & max lifespan by at least 100%
[Odds: 10%]

Simplifying a bit, your life expectancy could be 75*(1.725) = 
130 years.  Actually, the odds of successful gene therapy,
cell repair machines, etc. are much higher in the additional
39 years than in the previous 50, and higher in turn in the next
30, etc., so this could be quite a serious underestimate of
the mean.

In addition, reversible freezing & storage of intact drosophila
embryos (50,000 cells with differentiated nerves, muscle, etc.)
has been demonstrated, and techniques for reversible cryonics of
mammals may be demonstrated within the next 50 years, and this
is likely within the next 89 years with advanced biotech.  Successful 
reversal demonstrated on mammals will likely cause a great increase in the 
market, greatly increasing the security of cryonics organizations 
and reducing the odds of being thawed out by organizational failure 
or statist intervention, so perhaps overall increasing the odds of 
successful suspension to 50% or more.

Your comments on the current dismal state of cryonics are 
well-taken, especially having read the incredible bickering
in CryoNet the last few weeks. Pursuing cryonics in Asia
is a very interesting idea; perhaps Japanese culture may
be a fertile memetic soil.  They commonly store live fish
at near-freezing for shipment to sushi shops, which like
their sushi minutes-fresh, so their popular culture is 
already quite familiar with using cold for biostasis.
Japanese often mix&match their religion; weddings are
usually Shinto and funerals Bhuddist, but there is
a bit of Christian and quite a bit of secular tradition as
well.

For myself, I hope to have a sharp mind & a bit of wealth
at age 90, so that I can take full advantage of the techniques
that will be developed during the next several decades.
Meanwhile I will pursue other forms of "immortality" such
as raising children & posting memes on the net; no reason
to have a narrow focus when it comes to life.

