Subject: POLI/RELIG: Marxism the Lesser of Evils?

(My title will raise some eyebrows....)

Sameer Parekh, quoting Tom Morrow's comments on Owen Rowley's anti-Church
comments (whew!):

>> Owen's statement is surely false in quantitative terms, too.  Does the RC
>> demand anthing near 50% of its adherents' incomes?  Total tax burdens often
>> reach this figure.
>> 
>        It used to.
>        I think the point was the religion has been known to do such
>things. Right now the RC doesn't have such power, but when the RC was
>acting with more power than the states, the RC was just as or more
>coercive/oppresive than the states.

Visit Latin America for evidence of the Roman Catholic Church in all its
splendor. Peasants live in squalor while the local church has
jewel-encrusted crucifixes and golden baubles in abundance.

In many villages, failure to tithe results in ostracism.

(Ironically, as "force" is not involved (in the main), there is nothing
"wrong" with this situation, according to the principles most of us adhere
to. The ostracism of nonbelievers in small villages is not something we
would want to change, nor could we.)

And the Church is often in collusion with the large landowners (who I doubt
tithe very much each year!) and the State itself. As in feudal Europe, the
Church is used as an instrument of control. Peasants are kept in poverty,
the Church acts as the agent of the State (the king, princes, dictators,
etc.), and even things like the confessional have a role to play (strong
evidence that the practice of "confessing" emerged to consolidate priestly
power, to ensure they knew what was brewing in their villages, and to give
themselves more leverage).

Church = State = Feudalism = A Few Enormously Wealthy Landowners (in many
countries)

The Latin American experience tends to make me support forms of Marxist
revolution (gasp!)....the Marxists take the "land grants" (huge parcels of
land, millions of acres, given out by the Kings of Spain and Portugal and
with the support of the Pope to toadying sycophants in the 17th and 18th
centuries) away from the landowners and redistribute them (sometimes).
Then, some years later, the Marxists themselves get thrown out and replaced
by some form of a market economy. Sometimes land redistribution is needed.
That's a fact.

I don't claim to be a Marxist, natch. Nor do I support "land reform" in all
of its forms (as in Cambodia, the collectivizations in Russia and China,
etc.). I just note that sometime the fastest (best?) route from feudalistic
ownership of State/Church-granted estates to a more "reasonable" (= smaller
estates, free market in land ownership, etc.) system is through some form
of Marxist revolution. There may be other routes, free market routes, of
course. But I haven't seen many reported on.

Of the overthrow of Marxism in the East Bloc, it was written (by somebody):
"There have been a thousand books written about the transition from
capitalism to communism, but not a single book about the transition from
comunism to capitalism."

I'd like to hear ideas on how extremely concentrated land ownership, as
I've described to be the norm in much of Latin America, can be, or should
be, dealt with. Some of the ideas of Mark Miller about "Inheritance Day"
may be one place to start.

(Note: You won't get any points from me if you write about how nanotech
will make land ownership moot, how boundless expansion and an era of
abundance will...blah, blah, blah.)

Remember, before you flame me to a crisp, that I'm a free market advocate,
even an anarchocapitalist, and that I was raisd on Rand and Heinlein.

-Tim May, who fervently hopes _his_ land is not "redistributed"

